              "text": "To pay your federal taxes online, the safest and most direct way is through the official **Internal Revenue Service (IRS)** portal. Avoid search engine ads that may lead to unofficial third-party sites, and always ensure the URL starts with **`https://www.irs.gov`**.\n\nThe best online payment options depend on how you want to pay and whether you are paying personal or business taxes:\n\n---\n\n### 1. IRS Direct Pay (Best for Individuals \u2014 Free)\nThis is the most popular and recommended method for individuals to pay directly from a checking or savings account.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **No Account Required:** You do not need to register or log in. You just need to verify your identity using information from a prior year\u2019s tax return.\n* **Best for:** Paying your annual 1040 balance, quarterly estimated taxes, or filing extension payments.\n* **How to use:** Go to the official [IRS Direct Pay](https://www.irs.gov/payments/direct-pay) page, select \"Make a Payment,\" and choose your payment reason and tax year.\n\n### 2. Your IRS Online Account (Best for Managing Balance \u2014 Free)\nIf you want to view exactly what you owe before making a payment, you can log into your secure IRS Individual Online Account.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **Setup Required:** Requires verification through ID.me.\n* **Best for:** Taxpayers who want to view their payment history, check an outstanding balance, review a payment plan, or schedule future bank transfers.\n* **How to use:** Log in via the [IRS Online Account](https://www.irs.gov/your-online-account) portal.\n\n### 3. Debit Card, Credit Card, or Digital Wallet (Fees Apply)\nYou can pay using a credit card, debit card, or digital wallet (such as PayPal, Venmo, or Apple Pay) through one of the IRS's approved third-party payment processors.\n* **Cost:** **Fees apply.** \n  * Debit cards usually have a flat fee of around $2.00 to $2.50.\n  * Credit cards and digital wallets charge a percentage fee (typically 1.8% to 2.0% of your total payment).\n* **Approved Processors:** The IRS uses three official processors: *Pay1040*, *ACI Payments, Inc.*, and *Link2Gov*.\n* **How to use:** Access these processors safely via the [IRS Pay by Card](https://www.irs.gov/payments/pay-your-taxes-by-debit-or-credit-card) page to avoid scams.\n\n### 4. EFTPS: Electronic Federal Tax Payment System (Best for Businesses & Large Payments)\nThis is a secure government portal designed primarily for business taxes, payroll taxes, or individuals who make large, frequent estimated tax payments.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **Setup Required:** You must enroll ahead of time. Once you sign up, the IRS will mail you a personal identification number (PIN), which can take 5 to 7 business days.\n* **Best for:** Businesses, estimated quarterly taxes, and payments exceeding $10 million.\n* **How to use:** Visit the official [EFTPS website](https://www.eftps.gov).\n\n### 5. Electronic Funds Withdrawal (E-file Direct Pay)\nIf you are currently preparing your tax return using tax preparation software (like TurboTax, H&R Block, TaxSlayer, etc.), you do not need to go to a separate website to pay.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **How to use:** While e-filing your return, you can opt to pay via \"Electronic Funds Withdrawal\". Simply enter your bank routing and account numbers, select the date you want the payment debited, and the software will submit the payment instructions along with your tax return.\n\n---\n\n### What if you can\u2019t afford to pay the full amount?\nIf you cannot pay your federal tax bill in full, **you should still file your tax return on time to avoid late-filing penalties**. Once you file, you can apply for an online payment plan:\n* **Short-term payment plan:** Gives you up to 180 days to pay the balance (penalties and interest still accrue, but setup fees are minimal or waived).\n* **Installment Agreement (Long-term payment plan):** Allows you to make monthly payments for up to 72 months. \n* You can apply directly through the [IRS Online Payment Agreement](https://www.irs.gov/payments/online-payment-agreement-application) tool.",
